#original-page(595pt, 842pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.705pt, 804.24pt, "4 ")
  #line-at(74.88pt, 68.16pt, "In summary, while steady-state methods offer direct thermal conductivity measurements, they are ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 95.76pt, "less effective for thin films due to low thermal conductivity and small thicknesses. Transient ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 123.36pt, "techniques such as AC calorimetry and TDTR offer higher precision but typically require knowledge ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 150.96pt, "of the heat capacity, potentially limiting their accuracy. Therefore, there remains a critical need for ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 178.56pt, "an integrated, high-resolution method capable of simultaneously determining anisotropic thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 206.16pt, "conductivities and volumetric heat capacity in thin polymer films.  ")
  #line-at(74.88pt, 233.76pt, "To address this need, we introduce a non-contact Square-Pulsed Source (SPS) method for ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 261.36pt, "simultaneous measurement of in-plane and cross-plane thermal conductivities and volumetric heat ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 288.96pt, "capacity of thin films within a single experimental setup. The SPS method employs square-wave ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 316.56pt, "heating over a broad modulation frequency range (1 Hz to 10 MHz), combined with high-precision ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 344.16pt, "amplitude signal detection, ensuring robust and reliable measurements. In comparison, other ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 371.76pt, "thermoreflectance techniques, such as TDTR, are limited by a lowest measurable frequency of 0.1 ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 399.36pt, "MHz, which restricts their ability to accurately measure low in-plane thermal conductivity [18]. ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 426.96pt, "FDTR, on the other hand, employs sinusoidal modulation of the pump laser and relies on frequency-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 454.56pt, "dependent phase signals for parameter fitting. However, as the frequency decreases, these phase ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 482.16pt, "signals approach zero, making low-frequency signals ineffective for accurate parameter extraction ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 509.76pt, "[19]. In contrast, the SPS method is particularly suited for characterizing thin films with low thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 537.36pt, "conductivity, offering greater accuracy, enhanced sensitivity at low frequencies, and improved ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 564.96pt, "experimental flexibility.  ")
  #line-at(74.88pt, 592.56pt, "In this study, we apply the SPS method to characterize three suspended PI thin films (Kapton HN-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 620.16pt, "100 and EN-100, and a 15 μm Kaneka PI film) and three spin-coated PI films on fused silica substrates ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 648pt, "(4.45, 5.0, and 10 μm thick). Our results show that ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(297.922pt, 648pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.4pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(304.543pt, 650.4pt, "!")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(309.81pt, 648pt, " is similar for both film types, while ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(482.549pt, 648pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.4pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(489.17pt, 650.4pt, "\"")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(494.354pt, 648pt, " is higher ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 675.84pt, "in the spin-coated films, indicating structural differences when compared to the commercial products. ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 703.44pt, "The measured volumetric heat capacities are consistent across all samples, demonstrating the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 731.04pt, "accuracy and robustness of the SPS technique. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(56.88pt, 758.64pt, "2. Methodologies ")
]

