  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(252.12pt, 428.16pt, " of spin-coated PI films, compared to their suspended ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 456.24pt, "commercial counterparts, can be attributed to several morphological and interfacial factors. First, film ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 483.84pt, "morphology—particularly the density and continuity of polymer chains along the cross-plane ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 511.44pt, "direction—plays a significant role. Spin-coated films often undergo thermal curing, which can lead ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 539.04pt, "to denser packing and improved chain stacking perpendicular to the substrate, thereby facilitating ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 566.64pt, "phonon transport across layers. Second, inter-chain alignment induced during spin coating and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 594.24pt, "annealing may result in partial vertical ordering, especially when strong shear or solvent evaporation ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 621.84pt, "gradients are present during processing. This contrasts with commercial Kapton films, which are ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 649.44pt, "typically biaxially stretched and exhibit pronounced in-plane chain alignment, thereby reducing ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 677.04pt, "phonon pathways in the cross-plane direction. Finally, substrate influence cannot be overlooked. The ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 704.64pt, "rigid fused silica substrate used for spin-coated films may constrain polymer relaxation during curing, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 732.24pt, "suppressing void formation and improving interfacial adhesion. This intimate film–substrate contact ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 759.84pt, "can reduce thermal boundary resistance and enhance heat conduction from the metal transducer into ")
